ek minister sajin's claims about  how he handled the vance allegations  were thrown into question at committee  by expert testimony  the current military ombudsman backed  assertions of his predecessor  that sajin was the proper reporting  authority for concerns about general  vance  the head of the sensitive investigations  unit for the military police  also contradicts sagin's claim that  reporting the allegation  would have been political interference a  claim that sajin  repeated again today donna all right  mercedes stevenson in ottawa thank you  and one more military note a day before  canada's mission in iraq was set to  expire the federal government has  announced it is being extended  500 canadian troops are involved in  operation impact now the extension  allows that to increase up to 850 troops  if needed  until the end of next march canada has  been helping a coalition of countries  fight the so-called islamic state in  iraq and syria  since 2014. ahead heinous attacks on  asian americans prompt  hate crime investigations  police in new york are searching for  suspects who carried out brutal assaults  on asian americans  the latest happened in broad daylight in  midtown manhattan  a 65-year-old woman was kicked and  stomped on  it was caught on a security camera and a  warning the video is  disturbing it happened outside an  apartment building yesterday morning  a man approached the woman kicked her in  the stomach and repeatedly stomped on  her  before walking away she went to the  hospital with serious injuries  a security guard inside the lobby  watched it happen and did nothing  another man closed the door to the  building and left the woman  on the ground the building management  company says they  have been suspended the police hate  crimes unit is now  asking for public help in identifying  the suspect  and another attack on an asian-american  man is also under investigation he was  beaten on this busy subway train in  brooklyn  it's not clear when it took place or  what happened before the assault  we're not going to show you the whole  thing it's too violent the assailant  choked the man until he collapsed and  then  he walked off the train at the next stop  the nypd is asking the public for help  in that case too urging  anyone with information to come forward  in minneapolis the teenager who took the  video of george floyd's arrests and his  final moments  took the stand in the second day of the  murder trial of former police officer  derrick chauvin  she appeared off camera because she was  a minor at the time  in her testimony she described seeing  floyd as she says  terrified scared and begging for his  life eshovan pressed his knee on the 46  year old black man's neck  even though it was her video that  brought the world's attention to floyd's  death she says she  feels remorse for being unable to help  him  i have black i have a black father i  have a black brother i have black  friends  and i i look at that and i look at how  that could have been one of them  it's been nice i stayed up  apologizing  and apologizing  to george floyd for not doing more and  not physically interacting  and not saving his life but it's like  it's not what i should have done it's  what he should have done  is charged with second-degree murder  third degree murder and second-degree  manslaughter in connection with floyd's  death  the trial will continue for several more  weeks  virtual gurus still ahead the remote  recruiters thriving  in alberta  you're watching global national  a bright spot in the uk today it was the  warmest march day in 53 years  temperatures soared to above 24 degrees  in some places  it was convenient timing to enjoy the  outdoors strict lockdown measures eased  yesterday allowing people to gather  outside in groups of six and in japan  the cherry blossoms are bursting into  bloom earlier than any other year on  record  the city of kyoto has tracked the peak  bloom date for over 1200 years and says  spring flowering has been shifting  earlier over the past century  well finding moments of joy hasn't been  easy in the last year especially for  those who have been forced out of work  in alberta employment of women has been  driven down  to a low not seen in two decades it's a  harsh reality  a tech entrepreneur in calgary is  working to address  she has overcome a difficult past and as  heather urex west reports  she's now helping those who don't always  fit into a corporate box  bobby rossette came to calgary for the  opportunities  i moved here to work in oil and gas just  like a lot of people were moving out  here to get  oil and gas jobs at first the work was  steady  rissette was busy all the time but when  the price of oil collapsed in 2015  she and thousands of others were laid  off  at that time there's a lot of people in  calgary trying to find work  and there's just no opportunities for me  as an indigenous member of the lgbtq2  community  reset knew she was facing discrimination  too  it's something she had watched her two  moms struggle against for years  from a young age i've seen prejudices  against us as our family i've seen a lot  of  uh you know it was a lot of heartbreak  for us to deal with  that's essentially what compiled me to  say you know what i i got to be able to  build a platform that i can do this  and that i can provide work to those  that are going through the same thing as  i uh  doing good five years later reset is now  the ceo of a rapidly growing tech  startup  virtual gurus connects companies with  skilled assistants who work remotely  a model that is thriving during these  coveted times  i have like five six clients at the  moment and everyone's different  so every day is different no two days  are the same  stacy wells is one of 400 contractors  who is now working as a virtual  assistant because of assets company  most of the contractors are women many  like wells are indigenous and living in  remote communities  you know sometimes i surprise myself  though because i'll look online and i'll  look in in our community and i'll see  all the people that we're providing work  for and you know  it's kind of it brings a tear to my  sometimes this week the company closed  more than a million dollars in new  investment there are big plans for  growth  but for reset every new hire still feels  deeply personal you think back to your  mom yeah  yeah i always do i always think back to  what my mom went through  and that's what drives her to keep  striving for more  heather urex west global news calgary  and that is global national for this  tuesday i'm donna friesen tonight's your  canada is kinsman park south in regina  there are beautiful spots all over  canada please email yours to viewers at  globalnational.com  thanks for watching hope to see you here  again tomorrow take care of yourselves  [Music]  bye-bye
 

